// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"About 10,000 years ago, farming changed everything. It led to the first cities and civilizations.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The Neolithic Revolution (the shift to farming) let people settle in one place, grow food surpluses, and build cities."},{"t":"p","text":"The first civilizations grew in river valleys with fertile soil:"},{"t":"list","items":["Mesopotamia (Tigris and Euphrates): Sumerians invented cuneiform writing; Hammurabi created a famous law code.","Egypt (Nile): pharaohs, pyramids and hieroglyphics.","Indus Valley (Indus River): planned cities like Mohenjo-Daro.","China (Yellow River): dynasties ruled by the Mandate of Heaven."]},{"t":"p","text":"Civilizations share features: cities, government, social classes, specialized jobs, writing and religion."}]},{"say":"Greece and Rome created ideas about government, philosophy and law that still shape the world today.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Ancient Greece was made of independent city-states. Athens developed democracy, where citizens voted on laws. Philosophers like Socrates, Plato and Aristotle asked big questions about ethics and knowledge. Alexander the Great spread Greek culture across a huge empire."},{"t":"key","text":"Rome began as a republic, with elected senators, and later became an empire ruled by emperors. Roman law, engineering (roads, aqueducts) and the Latin language influenced Europe for centuries."},{"t":"p","text":"Christianity began in the Roman Empire and became its official religion. The Western Roman Empire fell in 476 CE, while the Eastern (Byzantine) Empire lasted until 1453."}]}],[{"say":"Religions shaped how billions of people live, think and govern. Let's meet the major world religions.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Judaism: monotheistic, based on the Torah and the covenant between God and the Hebrews.","Christianity: based on the teachings of Jesus; spread through the Roman Empire.","Islam: founded by Muhammad in Arabia in the 600s; the Quran and Five Pillars guide believers.","Hinduism: from India, with beliefs in karma, dharma and reincarnation.","Buddhism: founded by Siddhartha Gautama, teaching the Four Noble Truths and the Eightfold Path to end suffering.","Confucianism: Chinese philosophy emphasizing respect, family and social order."]},{"t":"key","text":"Religions spread along trade routes and through empires, and they shaped laws, art and daily life."}]},{"say":"After Rome fell, Europe and the Middle East developed in very different ways.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"In medieval Europe, feudalism organized society: kings gave land to lords in exchange for loyalty, and peasants (serfs) worked the land. The Catholic Church was the most powerful institution."},{"t":"p","text":"The Crusades (1096–1291) were religious wars between Christians and Muslims over the Holy Land. They increased trade and contact between Europe and the Middle East. The Black Death (1347–1351) killed about a third of Europe's population."},{"t":"p","text":"Meanwhile, the Islamic world experienced a Golden Age. Scholars in Baghdad made advances in math (algebra), medicine and astronomy, and preserved Greek knowledge."}]}],[{"say":"Starting in Italy around 1300, a rebirth of learning and art transformed Europe. Then the Church split apart.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The Renaissance revived interest in classical Greek and Roman ideas. Humanism focused on human potential and achievements."},{"t":"p","text":"Artists like Leonardo da Vinci and Michelangelo created masterpieces. Johannes Gutenberg's printing press (around 1450) spread books and ideas quickly."},{"t":"p","text":"In 1517, Martin Luther challenged the Catholic Church's practices, starting the Protestant Reformation. Protestant churches formed across northern Europe. The Catholic Church responded with the Counter-Reformation, and religious wars followed."},{"t":"oops","text":"The Reformation didn't end the Catholic Church. Europe became divided between Catholic and Protestant regions, and that split shaped wars and politics for over a century."},{"t":"tip","text":"Renaissance means \"rebirth,\" a reminder that it revived the learning of ancient Greece and Rome."}]},{"say":"European explorers crossed oceans in search of wealth, connecting the world as never before.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Europeans explored for \"God, gold and glory\": to spread Christianity, find wealth and trade routes, and gain power. New ships and navigation tools made it possible."},{"t":"p","text":"Columbus reached the Americas in 1492. Vasco da Gama sailed around Africa to India. Magellan's expedition sailed around the world."},{"t":"p","text":"European colonization brought the Columbian Exchange, the destruction of Native empires, and the Atlantic slave trade, which forcibly took millions of Africans to the Americas. Mercantilism shaped how colonial empires were run."}]}],[{"say":"New ways of thinking about nature and government led to revolutions that changed the world.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"The Scientific Revolution used observation and experiments: Copernicus and Galileo showed the Earth orbits the Sun, and Newton explained gravity."},{"t":"key","text":"Enlightenment thinkers applied reason to society. John Locke argued people have natural rights to life, liberty and property; Montesquieu proposed separation of powers."},{"t":"p","text":"These ideas inspired the American Revolution (1776), the French Revolution (1789) and the Haitian Revolution (1791), the first successful revolt by enslaved people. Revolutions in Latin America, led by Simón Bolívar and others, won independence from Spain."}]},{"say":"Machines and factories changed how people worked and lived, starting in Britain in the 1700s.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The Industrial Revolution began in Britain because it had coal, iron, rivers, money to invest and workers."},{"t":"p","text":"Steam engines powered factories, trains and ships. Textile mills mass-produced cloth. Cities grew rapidly as people moved for factory jobs, but workers faced long hours, child labor and crowded, dirty cities."},{"t":"p","text":"New ideas responded to these problems: labor unions fought for workers' rights, and Karl Marx proposed communism, a system where workers would control production. Industrialized nations also built empires in Africa and Asia to get raw materials and markets."}]}],[{"say":"Two world wars in the 20th century caused enormous destruction and reshaped the globe.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"World War I (1914–1918) was caused by militarism, alliances, imperialism and nationalism. The assassination of Archduke Franz Ferdinand sparked it. Trench warfare and new weapons killed millions."},{"t":"p","text":"The harsh Treaty of Versailles and the Great Depression helped dictators rise, including Hitler in Germany and Mussolini in Italy."},{"t":"p","text":"World War II (1939–1945) was the deadliest war in history. The Holocaust was the Nazi genocide of six million Jews and millions of others. The Allies defeated Germany and Japan, and the United Nations was created to keep peace."}]},{"say":"After World War II, the world split between two superpowers, colonies won independence, and the world grew more connected.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"The Cold War divided the world between the US (democracy and capitalism) and the Soviet Union (communism). It included an arms race, the space race, and proxy wars in Korea and Vietnam. It ended when the Soviet Union collapsed in 1991."},{"t":"p","text":"Decolonization: colonies in Asia and Africa won independence. India gained independence from Britain in 1947 through Gandhi's nonviolent movement; many African nations became independent in the 1950s and 1960s."},{"t":"p","text":"Today, globalization connects economies, cultures and people through trade, technology and the internet, while the world faces challenges like climate change and inequality."}]}]];
export default text;
